## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Classification

Your code is **Timeout enforcement** with a **ThreadAbortException/ResetAbort** catch block. Three APIs must go:

| Removed API | Why |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+ |
| `ThreadAbortException` catch | Never thrown anymore |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | No abort to reset |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException when the token fires
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Changes

1. **`CancellationTokenSource(timeout)`** replaces the `Thread.Join` + `Thread.Abort` timeout pattern. The token auto-cancels after the deadline.
2. **`Task.Run`** replaces manual `new Thread(...)`. The `CancellationToken` is passed so the scheduler can skip the work if already cancelled before it starts.
3. **`Task.WaitAsync(token)`** (.NET 6+) gives cancellable synchronous waiting — the caller unblocks immediately when the timeout fires.
4. **`catch (OperationCanceledException)`** replaces `catch (ThreadAbortException)`. The `when` guard ensures we only catch our own timeout, not unrelated cancellations.

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Work doesn't actually stop** | Cooperative cancellation means `Task.Run(work)` keeps running in the background after timeout. If `work` is a tight CPU loop, it won't stop unless it checks a `CancellationToken`. Consider changing the signature to `Func<CancellationToken, T>` and passing `cts.Token` into the work. |
| **Fire-and-forget background work** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, the orphaned task can hold resources (DB connections, file handles). Add `task.ContinueWith(t => Log(t.Exception), TaskContinuationOptions.OnlyOnFaulted)` to observe failures. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | `CTS` is `IDisposable` — leaking it leaks internal timers. The `using` statement handles this. |

### If You Need the Work to Actually Stop

Change the API to accept cooperative cancellation:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;

    try
    {
        var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);
        return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

Callers then check the token inside their work:

```csharp
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return result;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

This is the fundamental paradigm shift: **preemptive** (runtime injects exception) → **cooperative** (code voluntarily checks). Without checkpoints in the work delegate, the background task will run to completion even after the caller gets `TimeoutException`.
